From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 16:06:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] msgblock: Declare resync state before label

The bounded resynchronization path introduced a next_sync declaration immediately after the resync label.  A C label must precede a statement, while a declaration is a block item and did not become labelable until the newer C language rules.  Recent host GCC accepts this placement as an extension, which allowed the normal development build to pass, but the GCC 8 toolchain on an existing Klipper host rejects c_helper.so with 'a label can only be part of a statement'.

Declare the pointer at function scope and assign it after the label.  This retains the single resynchronization path and the exact memchr bounds, discard count, need_sync transition, and return values while making the helper valid for older supported host compilers.

The host helper rebuild and a direct GNU C99 compilation of msgblock.c complete successfully.  No MCU code, message format, or runtime behavior changes.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/klippy/chelper/msgblock.c b/klippy/chelper/msgblock.c
index 12226631..4b2d7229 100644
--- a/klippy/chelper/msgblock.c
+++ b/klippy/chelper/msgblock.c
@@ -49,6 +49,7 @@ msgblock_finalize(uint8_t *buf, uint8_t msg_len, uint8_t sequence)
 int
 msgblock_check(uint8_t *need_sync, const uint8_t *buf, int buf_len)
 {
+    const uint8_t *next_sync;
     if (buf_len < MESSAGE_MIN)
         // Need more data
         return 0;
@@ -74,7 +75,7 @@ msgblock_check(uint8_t *need_sync, const uint8_t *buf, int buf_len)
 
 resync:
     // Discard through the next sync byte before testing another frame
-    const uint8_t *next_sync = memchr(buf, MESSAGE_SYNC, buf_len);
+    next_sync = memchr(buf, MESSAGE_SYNC, buf_len);
     if (next_sync) {
         *need_sync = 0;
         return -(next_sync - buf + 1);
